You are a security architect analyzing an AI/LLM system description within an STPA-Sec (System-Theoretic Process Analysis for Security) framework.

Your task is to extract a capability profile that captures the system's structural properties. This profile will be used to derive a control structure that prevents the losses and hazards identified in the loss analysis.

## Schneider zones (for entry-point annotation)
- "input" — Input Surfaces (user prompts, file uploads, API calls)
- "reasoning" — Planning & Reasoning (LLM inference, chain-of-thought)
- "tool_execution" — Tool Execution (API calls, code execution, database access)
- "memory" — Memory & State (vector stores, knowledge graphs, persistent storage)
- "inter_agent" — Inter-Agent Communication (agent-to-agent messaging)

## Rules
- entry_points: list of entry point objects, each with "name", "direction", and optionally "controllability" fields.
  - name: short description of the entry point.
  - direction: one of "input", "output", or "bidirectional".
  - controllability: one of "direct", "indirect", "system", or null.
- confidence: "high" if the description is detailed, "medium" if moderate, "low" if vague.
- kc_subcodes: OWASP KC sub-codes identifying the system's capabilities. Select ALL that apply.
- tool_inventory: tools and APIs the system can invoke. Be thorough — these feed the completeness critic.

## Emphasis

Since this profile feeds a control structure completeness critic, emphasize:
- **Completeness of tool inventory**: every tool/API the system can invoke must be listed.
- **Completeness of entry points**: every attacker-accessible ingress path must be identified.
- **Accurate KC sub-codes**: these determine which taxonomy-derived probes the critic applies.

The loss analysis context helps you understand what losses and hazards the capability profile needs to support.
